- `action(n5, "Send a Denial to the Customer due to Credit Limit")`, `action(n7, "Send a Denial to the Customer due to Article Unavailability")`: Lack outgoing precedence relations, leaving both denial paths as dead ends. The source implies these scenarios should reach a terminal state, violating SMC1/SMC5.
- `precedence(e1, c3)`: Incorrectly routes control flow out of an `end` node. By definition, `end` nodes must terminate execution; routing `e1` forward corrupts control-flow logic and violates SMC4.
- `converge(c3)`, `precedence(n20, c3)`, `precedence(c3, n23)`: Merges the "Send a Reminder" path with the closure sequence, forcing it through `action(n23, "End process after Closing Order")`. The source explicitly restricts order closure to the write-off branch ("After writing off the Order, close the Order..."), so merging the reminder into this sequence hallucinates behavior and breaks chronology (SMC1, SMC3, SMC5).
- `precedence(e1, c3)`, `precedence(c3, n23)`, `precedence(n23, e2)`: Artificially funnels the initial payment-success path (`e1`) through a convergence and mandatory final closure step, contradicting the source's direct instruction to simply "close the Order and end the process" upon payment receipt (SMC5).

Structural Check
- [S1] STC6 failed: End nodes must not have outgoing precedence edges. | Violations: e1 | DSL: end(e1)
- [S2] STC8 failed: Each action node must have exactly one incoming and one outgoing edge. | Node `n5` has in=1 and out=0. | Node `n7` has in=1 and out=0. | DSL: action(n5,"Send a Denial to the Customer due to Credit Limit"); action(n7,"Send a Denial to the Customer due to Article Unavailability")
- [S3] STC20 failed: Every non-end node must have a path to at least one end node. | Violations: n5, n7 | DSL: action(n5,"Send a Denial to the Customer due to Credit Limit"); action(n7,"Send a Denial to the Customer due to Article Unavailability")